WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.720
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:11.120
Today we are going to do this, and this, and this.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:14.320
One of these applications is called a NERF.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.560
What is that? NERFs mean that we have&nbsp;
a collection of photos like these,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:28.240
and magically, create a video where we can fly&nbsp;
through these photos. Yes, typically, scientists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:35.120
now use some sort of learning-based AI method to&nbsp;
fill in all this information between these photos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:41.760
Something that sounded like science fiction&nbsp;
just a couple years ago, and now, here we are.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.680
Now, these are mostly learning-based&nbsp;
methods, therefore, these techniques need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:54.480
some training time. Wanna see how their&nbsp;
results evolve over time? I surely do, so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:00.960
let’s have a look together! This NERF paper was&nbsp;
published about a year and a half or two years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:07.680
ago. We typically have to wait for at least a&nbsp;
few hours for something to happen. Then came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:14.720
the Plenoxels paper with something that looks&nbsp;
like black magic. Yes, that’s right, this trains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:22.880
in a matter of minutes. And it was published two&nbsp;
months ago. Such improvement, in just two years.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:30.800
But, here is NVIDIA’s new paper from&nbsp;
about a month ago. And I hear you asking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:36.800
Károly, are you telling me that a two month&nbsp;
old paper of this caliber is going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.960
outperformed by a one month old paper?&nbsp;
Yes, that is exactly what I am saying.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:49.120
Now, hold on to your papers, and look here,&nbsp;
with the new method, the training takes…what?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:57.200
Less time than I have to utter this&nbsp;
sentence, because it is already done. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:05.520
first, we wait from hours to days, then,&nbsp;
2 years later, it trains in minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:14.400
and a month later, just a month later, it trains&nbsp;
in a couple seconds. Basically, nearly instantly.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:21.360
And, if we let it run for a bit longer, but still&nbsp;
less than two minutes, it will not only outperform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:27.120
a naive technique, but will provide better&nbsp;
quality results than a previous method,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:34.720
while training for about ten times quicker.&nbsp;
That is absolutely incredible. I would say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:38.160
that this is swift progress&nbsp;
in machine learning research,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.880
but that word will not cut it&nbsp;
here. This is truly something else.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:48.960
But, if that wasn’t enough, NERFing&nbsp;
is not the only thing this one can do.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:57.680
It can also approximate a gigapixel image. What&nbsp;
is that? That is an image with tons of data in it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:04.160
and the AI is asked to create a cheaper&nbsp;
neural representation of this image. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:10.000
we can just keep zooming in, and zooming&nbsp;
in, and we still find new details there.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:15.920
Now if you have been holding on to your papers&nbsp;
so far, now, squeeze that paper, because what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:24.400
you see here is not the result, but the whole&nbsp;
training process itself. Really? Yes, really.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:31.040
Did you see it? Well, did you blink? Because&nbsp;
if you did, you almost certainly missed it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:38.320
This was also trained from scratch, right in front&nbsp;
of our eyes. But it’s so quick, that if you take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:45.280
just a moment to hold on to your papers a bit more&nbsp;
tightly, and you already missed it. Once again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:52.400
a couple papers before, this took several&nbsp;
hours at the very least. That is outstanding.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:59.920
And if we were done here, I would be very happy.&nbsp;
But, we are not done yet, not even close! It can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:08.080
still do two more amazing things. One, this is a&nbsp;
neural signed distance field it has produced. That&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:14.640
is a mapping from 3D coordinates in a virtual&nbsp;
world to distance to a surface. Essentially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:21.200
it learns the geometry of the object better&nbsp;
because it knows what parts are inside, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:27.840
outside. And it is blazing fast, surprisingly,&nbsp;
even for objects with detailed geometry.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:36.320
And, my favorite, it can also do neural radiance&nbsp;
caching. What is that? At the risk of simplifying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:42.080
the problem, essentially, it is learning to&nbsp;
perform a light transport simulation. It took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:49.200
me several years of research to be able to produce&nbsp;
such a light simulation, so let’s see how long it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:05:01.120
takes for the AI to do. Well…let’s see…holy mother&nbsp;
of papers! NVIDIA, what are you doing! I give up.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:05.440
As you see, the pace of progress in&nbsp;
AI and computer graphics research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:11.280
is absolutely incredible, and even&nbsp;
better, it is accelerating over time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:17.200
Things that were wishful thinking 10&nbsp;
years ago become not only possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:25.520
but are now easy over the span of just a couple&nbsp;
of papers. I am stunned. What a time to be alive!

